    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d106">

                <pb facs="0193" n="89" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg89"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3689: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-01-29T07:00:00+08:00">January 29, 1932—7
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 28—11:28 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Japanese Navy with slight resistance occupied Chapei about midnight while all
                    were lulled into a [sense?] of relief by the assurance of <pb facs="0194" n="90"
                        xml:id="pg90"/> Japanese to full [<hi rendition="#italic">consular?</hi>]
                    body at 4 o’clock yesterday afternoon. The troop movements are stated to be on
                    the ground of protection of large number Japanese in Chapei. Occasional heavy
                    artillery is heard in that direction as well as planes plainly visible. Details
                    will follow.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Department [<hi rendition="#italic">Legation?</hi>].</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
